  "query_preview": "..\nGenerally sunny skies with clouds over the higher terrain across\nSoutheast Arizona. Winds have been breezy to windy for first\nhalf of the day with Douglas Airport already reached a max gust\nof 40 mph and Tucson Airport of 41 mph. Expect the breezy to \nwindy conditions to continue through the early afternoon. A Wind\nAdvisory is still in effect through 2pm, to highlight the \nstronger breezes. Winds will be breezy for the remainder of the \nday and into tomorrow, but lower in speed than this morning. \n\nWater vapor satellite shows atmospheric moisture nudging into\nthe southern portion of the region. This will increase cloud\ncover for areas along the International Border starting Monday\nafternoon. Only a few hi-res models, such as the HRRR, is \nadvertising some rain shower activity by Organ Pipe to Ajo. \nThere is not much of a mechanism to support shower activity as \nwe get into the more of the cooler season; sun angle is there \nas it was in early September. Chances for any activity is less \nthan 10 percent and may likely result in cloud buildups Monday \nafternoon. \n\nHigh pressure will stay around for the most of the week. As the\nhigh shifts around, its flow will bring some moisture and a\nminor shortwave into the eastern half of the county warning \narea on Wednesday. This will provide slight chances for rain \nshowers and thunderstorms in the mountains and the eastern \nvalleys. \n\nFor the rest of the week, a surface pressure gradient will \nreform, bringing another round of easterly breezes starting on \nThursday. Ensemble model members are consistent on a pattern\nchange by the weekend to early next week. A trough will be \nforming along the western coast, but there is uncertainty on \nthe depth and the timing of the trough. For now, there are \nchances for rain showers towards the end of the forecast period.\nBeyond, CPC outlooks has 40-50% lean for above normal \nprecipitation and 50-60% likely above normal temperatures for \nthe 8-14 day period.",
